detail.abstract:
Objectives: The contraction of legacy public transit networks, particularly intercity bus systems, necessitates structural interventions to sustain regional connectivity. This study proposes a methodological framework to integrate Regional Air Mobility (RAM) into aging ground transportation infrastructure through the adaptive reuse of stranded transit facilities, operated as a public demand-responsive service rather than a premium air-taxi product.&nbsp; 
<div>
 Methods: Utilizing complex network theory and General Transit Feed Specification (GTFS) data, a multistage topology optimization process is developed. The framework employs consensus clustering based on the Leiden algorithm and centrality profiling (PageRank and HITS) to objectively identify structural vulnerabilities and designate legacy terminals as regional RAM hubs. A demand-responsive transit (DRT) RAM overlay is generated to bridge identified peripheral nodes with the central network. The framework is demonstrated on the South Korean intercity bus network across 110 experimental scenarios.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Findings: Under the selected configuration (𝛼 = 0.8, PageRank, 30% inclusion), 404 aerial links reduce the effective network diameter from 3 to 2 and average travel time by 46%, a topological upper bound that remains at 43% after screening out metropolitan-rail satellite nodes. Across the 110-scenario space, reductions reach up to 54%. Furthermore, the augmented multimodal network exhibits enhanced structural robustness against targeted node disruptions, delaying fragmentation under the sequential removal of critical hubs.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Novelty: This work reframes decommissioned transit terminals as strategically located stranded assets and provides a transferable, data-driven topology optimization procedure for converting them into vertiports that anchor a public demand-responsive RAM overlay.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Practical Applications: The findings support the feasibility of retrofitting existing transport nodes as vertiports, providing regional planners with a decision-support tool for deploying scalable and resilient aerial mobility systems while circumventing the prohibitive capital costs of greenfield vertiport development.
</div>
